, C is not always true.\n\nOption D: cos x > sin\u00b2 x.\n\nWe need to check if cos x > (sin x)^2 for all x in the interval. Let's analyze this.\n\nAgain, since x is in (-7\u03c0/4, -3\u03c0/2), equivalent to \u03b8 = x + 2\u03c0 in (\u03c0/4, \u03c0/2). So, cos \u03b8 > sin\u00b2 \u03b8.\n\nLet's express this as cos \u03b8 - sin\u00b2 \u03b8 > 0. Recall that sin\u00b2 \u03b8 = 1 - cos\u00b2 \u03b8. Therefore:\n\ncos \u03b8 - (1 - cos\u00b2 \u03b8) = cos \u03b8 - 1 + cos\u00b2 \u03b8 = cos\u00b2 \u03b8 + cos \u03b8 - 1.\n\nLet\u2019s denote y = cos \u03b8. Then the expression becomes y\u00b2 + y - 1.\n\nWe need to check if y\u00b2 + y - 1 > 0 for y in (0, \u221a2/2). Because \u03b8 is between \u03c0/4 and \u03c0/2, so cos \u03b8 decreases from \u221a2/2 to 0. Therefore, y = cos \u03b8 \u2208 (0, \u221a2/2).\n\nLet\u2019s solve the inequality y\u00b2 + y - 1 > 0.\n\nThe quadratic equation y\u00b2 + y - 1 = 0 has roots at y = [-1 \u00b1 \u221a(1 + 4)] / 2 = [-1 \u00b1 \u221a5]/2. The positive root is (-1 + \u221a5)/2 \u2248 ( -1 + 2.236 ) / 2 \u2248 1.236 / 2 \u2248 0.618. The negative root is irrelevant since y > 0.\n\nSo, the inequality y\u00b2 + y - 1 > 0 holds when y > 0.618 or y < (-1 - \u221a5)/2 \u2248 -1.618. Since y = cos \u03b8 \u2208 (0, \u221a2/2 \u2248 0.707), we compare 0.618 and 0.707.\n\nSince 0.618 < 0.707, the inequality y\u00b2 + y - 1 > 0 holds when y > 0.618 in the interval (0, 0.707). Therefore, for y \u2208 (0.618, 0.707), the inequality holds, but for y \u2208 (0, 0.618), it does not.\n\nTherefore, in terms of \u03b8, cos \u03b8 > 0.618 corresponds to \u03b8 < arccos(0.618) \u2248 51.9 degrees (since cos 51.9\u00b0 \u2248 0.618). Since \u03b8 is between \u03c0/4 (45 degrees) and \u03c0/2 (90 degrees), the inequality holds when \u03b8 < 51.9 degrees, i.e., in the interval (45\u00b0, 51.9\u00b0), and doesn't hold when \u03b8 > 51.9\u00b0, i.e., in (51.9\u00b0, 90\u00b0). Therefore, cos x > sin\u00b2 x is not always true for all x in (-7\u03c0/4,