ing that halving demand means the quantity demanded at each price is half, so Qd_spring = 0.5*(a - bP).\n\nBut we also know that when demand is halved (in spring), the equilibrium quantity decreases by 1.5 times. So Q_spring = Q_summer / 1.5.\n\nAlso, in spring, total revenue is P_spring * Q_spring = 90.\n\nIn summer, equilibrium is where Qd_summer = Qs_summer.\n\nSimilarly, in spring, equilibrium is Qd_spring = Qs_spring. But since supply in spring is same as summer, Qs_spring = Qs_summer = c + dP (for P > 4).\n\nBut wait, supply in spring is same as summer. So the supply function doesn't change between spring and summer. Only demand changes. Therefore, in summer:\n\nEquilibrium: a - bP = c + dP.\n\nIn spring:\n\nEquilibrium: 0.5*(a - bP) = c + dP.\n\nAnd we know that Q_spring = Q_summer / 1.5.\n\nLet me denote Q_summer as Qs and Q_spring as Qp.\n\nSo Qp = Qs / 1.5.\n\nBut Qs = a - bP_summer = c + dP_summer.\n\nQp = 0.5*(a - bP_spring) = c + dP_spring.\n\nAlso, Qp = Qs / 1.5.\n\nSo we have:\n\nFrom summer equilibrium:\n\na - bP_summer = c + dP_summer \u2192 (1)\n\nFrom spring equilibrium:\n\n0.5*(a - bP_spring) = c + dP_spring \u2192 (2)\n\nAlso, Qp = Qs / 1.5 \u2192 (c + dP_spring) = (a - bP_summer)/1.5 \u2192 (3)\n\nAnd revenue in spring: P_spring * Qp = 90 \u2192 P_spring * (c + dP_spring) = 90 \u2192 (4)\n\nAdditionally, the winter scenario: supply is halved (Qs_winter = 0.5*(c + dP)), demand is half of spring demand (which is 0.5*(a - bP)), so Qd_winter = 0.25*(a - bP) [since spring demand is half of summer, and winter demand is half of spring, so quarter of summer]. Wait, the problem states: \"demand falls to half of the spring demand\". So spring demand is half of summer, winter demand is half of spring, so winter demand is 0.5 * 0.5 = 0.25 of summer demand. So Qd_winter = 0.25*(a - bP).\n\nBut supply in winter is halved compared to other seasons. So Qs_winter = 0.5*(c + dP).\n\nEquilibrium in winter: 0.25*(a - bP_winter) = 0.5*(c + dP_winter). And the quantity sold is 5 units. So:\n\n0.25*(a - bP_winter) = 0.5*(c + dP_winter) = 5.\n\nTherefore:\n\n0.25*(a - bP_winter) = 5 \u2192 a - bP_winter = 20 \u2192 (5)\n\n0.5*(c + dP_winter) = 5 \u2192 c + dP_winter = 10 \u2192 (6)\n\nAlso, since producers only produce if P > 4, we know that in all seasons, the equilibrium price must be greater than 4.\n\nNow, we have equations (1), (2), (3), (4), (5), (6). Let's count the variables:\n\nVariables are: a, b, c, d, P_summer, P_spring, P_winter.\n\nBut we have 6 equations. Let's see if we can solve for these variables.\n\nFirst, from equations (5) and (6):\n\nFrom (5): a - bP_winter = 20\n\nFrom (6): c + dP_winter = 10\n\nSo, we can express a and c in terms of b, d, and P_winter:\n\na = 20 + bP_winter\n\nc = 10 - dP_winter\n\nNow, substitute a and c into equations (1) and (2):\n\nEquation (1): a - bP_summer = c + dP_summer\n\nSubstitute a and c:\n\n(20 + bP_winter) - bP_summer = (10 - dP_winter) + dP_summer\n\nSimplify:\n\n20 + bP_winter - bP_summer = 10 - dP_winter + dP_summer\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n20 - 10 + bP_winter - bP_summer + dP_winter - dP_summer = 0\n\n10 + (b + d)P_winter - (b + d)P_summer = 0\n\nFactor:\n\n10 + (b + d)(P_winter - P_summer) = 0\n\nSo,\n\n(b + d)(P_winter - P_summer) = -10 \u2192 (1a)\n\nEquation (2): 0.5*(a - bP_spring) = c + dP_spring\n\nSubstitute a and c:\n\n0.5*(20 + bP_winter - bP_spring) = 10 - dP_winter + dP_spring\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n20 + bP_winter - bP_spring = 20 - 2dP_winter + 2dP_spring\n\nSimplify:\n\n20 + bP_winter - bP_spring - 20 + 2dP_winter - 2dP_spring = 0\n\n(bP_winter + 2dP_winter) - (bP_spring + 2dP_spring) = 0\n\nFactor:\n\n(b + 2d)P_winter - (b + 2d)P_spring = 0\n\nSo,\n\n(b + 2d)(P_winter - P_spring) = 0 \u2192 (2a)\n\nAssuming b + 2d \u2260 0, then P_winter = P_spring. But is this possible? Let's think.\n\nIf (b + 2d)(P_winter - P_spring) = 0, then either b + 2d = 0 or P_winter = P_spring.\n\nIf b + 2d = 0, then b = -2d. But since demand and supply slopes are typically positive (demand slopes downward, so b is positive; supply slopes upward, so d is positive). If b = -2d, then b would be negative if d is positive, which contradicts the demand slope. Therefore, we must have P_winter = P_spring.\n\nSo P_winter = P_spring.\n\nNow, from equation (4): P_spring * (c + dP_spring) = 90\n\nBut c = 10 - dP_winter, and P_winter = P_spring, so c = 10 - dP_spring.\n\nTherefore, substitute c into equation (4):\n\nP_spring * (10 - dP_spring + dP_spring) = 90 \u2192 P_spring * 10 = 90 \u2192 P_spring = 9.\n\nTherefore, P_spring = 9, and since P_winter = P_spring, P_winter = 9.\n\nBut wait, in winter, the price is 9. However, the problem states that in winter, when supply is halved and demand is half of spring, the quantity sold is 5. But we also need to check if the price makes sense. Producers only produce if P > 4. Here, 9 > 4, so that's okay.\n\nNow, from equation (6): c + dP_winter = 10. Since P_winter = 9,\n\nc + 9d = 10 \u2192 c = 10 - 9d.\n\nFrom equation (5): a - bP_winter = 20. P_winter = 9,\n\na - 9b = 20 \u2192 a = 20 + 9b.\n\nNow, go back to equation (1a):\n\n(b + d)(P_winter - P_summer) = -10.\n\nWe know P_winter = 9, so:\n\n(b + d)(9 - P_summer) = -10 \u2192 (b + d)(P_summer - 9) = 10.\n\nEquation (3): (c + dP_spring) = (a - bP_summer)/1.5.\n\nBut c = 10 - 9d, P_spring = 9, so c + d*9 = 10 - 9d + 9d = 10.\n\nTherefore, left side of equation (3) is 10 = (a - bP_summer)/1.5.\n\nThus,\n\na - bP_summer = 15.\n\nBut a = 20 + 9b, so:\n\n20 + 9b - bP_summer = 15 \u2192 9b - bP_summer = -5 \u2192 b(9 - P_summer) = -5 \u2192 b(P_summer - 9) = 5.\n\nSo we have:\n\nFrom equation (1a):\n\n(b + d)(P_summer - 9) = 10.\n\nFrom the above equation:\n\nb(P_summer - 9) = 5.\n\nLet me denote (P_summer - 9) = X.\n\nThen:\n\nbX = 5\n\nand\n\n(b + d)X = 10.\n\nSubstituting X = 5/b into the second equation:\n\n(b + d)*(5/b) = 10 \u2192 (5(b + d))/b = 10 \u2192 5 + 5d/b = 10 \u2192 5d/b = 5 \u2192 d/b = 1 \u2192 d = b.\n\nSo d = b.\n\nTherefore, from d = b, and we have earlier expressions:\n\nc = 10 - 9d = 10 - 9b.\n\na = 20 + 9b.\n\nAlso, from equation (1):\n\nFrom summer equilibrium:\n\na - bP_summer = c + dP_summer.\n\nBut d = b, c = 10 - 9b, a = 20 + 9b.\n\nSubstitute into equation (1):\n\n20 + 9b - bP_summer = 10 - 9b + bP_summer.\n\nSimplify:\n\n20 + 9b - bP_summer = 10 - 9b + bP_summer\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n20 + 9b - bP_summer -10 +9b - bP_summer =0\n\n10 + 18b - 2bP_summer =0\n\nDivide by 2:\n\n5 + 9b - bP_summer =0\n\nRearrange:\n\n9b - bP_summer = -5\n\nFactor:\n\nb(9 - P_summer) = -5 \u2192 which is the same as before, since b(P_summer -9)=5.\n\nSince d = b, and we have from equation (1a):\n\n(b + d)(P_summer -9) =10 \u2192 (b + b)(P_summer -9)=10 \u2192 2b(P_summer -9)=10 \u2192 b(P_summer -9)=5.\n\nWhich is consistent with our previous result.\n\nSo, now, we can find P_summer.\n\nFrom b(P_summer -9)=5 and from equation (3): a - bP_summer =15.\n\nBut a =20 +9b.\n\nSo:\n\n20 +9b -bP_summer =15 \u21929b -bP_summer= -5 \u2192 b(9 -P_summer)= -5 \u2192 same as before.\n\nSo we have:\n\nb(P_summer -9)=5 \u2192 from this, P_summer -9=5/b.\n\nBut also from the above, b(9 -P_summer)= -5 \u2192 same as.\n\nLet me see if we can find numerical values.\n\nWe have two equations:\n\n1. d = b\n\n2. c =10 -9b\n\n3. a =20 +9b\n\nAlso, we need to find P_summer.\n\nBut we have:\n\nFrom equation (3):\n\na - bP_summer =15 \u219220 +9b -bP_summer =15 \u21929b -bP_summer= -5 \u2192b(9 -P_summer)= -5 \u2192 from which:\n\nP_summer =9 +5/b.\n\nBut we also have from equation (1a):\n\n(b + d)(P_summer -9)=10 \u2192 since d =b,\n\n(2b)(P_summer -9)=10 \u2192 P_summer -9=10/(2b)=5/b.\n\nTherefore, P_summer=9 +5/b.\n\nWhich matches the previous expression. Therefore, this is consistent.\n\nBut we need another equation to solve for b.\n\nWait, perhaps we can use the summer equilibrium equation (1):\n\na -bP_summer =c +dP_summer\n\nSubstitute a, c, d:\n\n20 +9b -bP_summer =10 -9b +bP_summer.\n\nBring all terms to left:\n\n20 +9b -bP_summer -10 +9b -bP_summer =0 \u219210 +18b -2bP_summer=0.\n\nDivide by 2:\n\n5 +9b -bP_summer=0 \u2192 from here:\n\nbP_summer=5 +9b.\n\nBut from P_summer=9 +5/b:\n\nMultiply both sides by b:\n\nbP_summer=9b +5.\n\nWhich matches the above equation: bP_summer=5 +9b.\n\nTherefore, everything is consistent, but we still don't have a numerical value for b. So we need another equation.\n\nWait, perhaps we can use the fact that the supply curve starts at P=4. Since producers won't produce if P \u22644, the supply curve has a minimum price of 4. Therefore, the supply function is Qs = 0 when P \u22644, and Qs = m(P -4) when P >4, where m is the slope. Therefore, the supply function can be written as Qs = m(P -4). Similarly, demand functions are Qd = a -bP.\n\nTherefore, the summer supply is Qs_summer = m(P -4) for P >4.\n\nSimilarly, winter supply is Qs_winter =0.5*m(P -4) for P >4.\n\nBut in our previous notation, we wrote the supply function as Qs =c +dP. However, considering the minimum price, the supply function should actually be Qs = m(P -4) = mP -4m. Therefore, c = -4m and d =m. Therefore, Qs = -4m +mP.\n\nBut in our previous equations, we have c =10 -9d. But if c = -4m and d =m, then:\n\nc = -4d =10 -9d \u2192 -4d =10 -9d \u2192 5d=10 \u2192d=2.\n\nTherefore, solving:\n\nFrom c =10 -9d and c =-4d,\n\nSet -4d =10 -9d \u2192 5d=10 \u2192d=2.\n\nTherefore, d=2, then c=10 -9*2=10-18=-8.\n\nBut since supply is Qs =c +dP, and c=-8, d=2. But Qs =-8 +2P.\n\nHowever, supply is only active when P >4. Let's check if at P=4, Qs= -8 +8=0. Yes, which matches the condition. So for P >4, Qs=-8 +2P.\n\nTherefore, supply function in summer (and spring, autumn) is Qs = -8 +2P for P >4, 0 otherwise.\n\nSimilarly, winter supply is halved: Qs_winter =0.5*(-8 +2P)= -4 +P for P >4, 0 otherwise.\n\nNow, check if this fits with previous equations.\n\nFrom earlier, we had c =10 -9d. If d=2, c=10 -18= -8. Correct.\n\nAlso, a=20 +9b. But we need to find a and b.\n\nFrom equation (5): a -bP_winter=20. But P_winter=9.\n\nSo a -9b=20.\n\nBut a=20 +9b, so substituting into equation (5):\n\n20 +9b -9b=20 \u219220=20. Which is always true, so no new information.\n\nWe need another equation to find b.\n\nWait, summer demand is Qd_summer =a -bP.\n\nAnd summer equilibrium is Qd_summer = Qs_summer.\n\nSo:\n\na -bP_summer = -8 +2P_summer.\n\nBut a=20 +9b, so:\n\n20 +9b -bP_summer =-8 +2P_summer.\n\nRearranged:\n\n20 +9b +8 =bP_summer +2P_summer.\n\n28 +9b =P_summer(b +2).\n\nBut from earlier, we have P_summer=9 +5/b.\n\nSubstitute:\n\n28 +9b = (9 +5/b)(b +2).\n\nMultiply out the right side:\n\n9(b +2) +5/b(b +2)=9b +18 +5 +10/b=9b +23 +10/b.\n\nSo:\n\n28 +9b =9b +23 +10/b.\n\nSubtract 9b from both sides:\n\n28=23 +10/b.\n\nSubtract 23:\n\n5=10/b \u2192b=10/5=2.\n\nSo b=2.\n\nTherefore, d=2 (from earlier), b=2.\n\nThus, a=20 +9b=20 +18=38.\n\nTherefore, summer demand is Qd_summer=38 -2P.\n\nSpring demand is Qd_spring=0.5*(38 -2P)=19 -P.\n\nSupply in spring/summer/autumn is Qs=-8 +2P.\n\nNow, let's check if this fits.\n\nFirst, summer equilibrium:\n\nQd_summer=38 -2P = Qs_summer=-8 +2P.\n\n38 -2P =-8 +2P \u219238 +8=4P \u219246=4P \u2192P=11.5.\n\nSo P_summer=11.5, Q_summer=38 -2*11.5=38-23=15. Also, Qs_summer=-8 +2*11.5=23-8=15. Correct.\n\nSpring equilibrium:\n\nQd_spring=19 -P, Qs_spring=-8 +2P.\n\nSet equal:19 -P=-8 +2P \u219219 +8=3P \u219227=3P \u2192P=9. So P_spring=9.\n\nQ_spring=-8 +2*9=10. Which matches earlier result (Qs=10). And revenue is 9*10=90, which matches the given condition. Also, Q_spring=10, which should be Q_summer/1.5=15/1.5=10. Correct.\n\nWinter scenario:\n\nDemand is half of spring demand, so Qd_winter=0.5*(19 -P)=9.5 -0.5P.\n\nSupply is halved: Qs_winter=-4 +P.\n\nSet equal:9.5 -0.5P =-4 +P \u21929.5 +4=1.5P \u219213.5=1.5P \u2192P=9. Which matches P_winter=9.\n\nQ_winter=-4 +9=5. Correct.\n\nAll conditions are satisfied.\n\nNow, we have all parameters:\n\n- Summer demand: Qd =38 -2P\n- Spring demand: Qd=19 -P\n- Winter demand: Qd=9.5 -0.5P\n- Autumn demand: Same as spring? No, demand varies each season, but supply is same in spring, summer, autumn. The problem says \"demand varies in each season\", so autumn demand is different from spring and summer. However, we weren't given specific info about autumn, so maybe it's not needed for the questions.\n\nBut the questions are about summer and possibly other seasons.\n\nNow, the questions:\n\n1) If the price is frozen at the winter price (which is 9) for the entire year, what will be the deficit in summer?\n\nDeficit occurs when price is below equilibrium, leading to Qd > Qs. In summer, the equilibrium price is 11.5. If the price is frozen at 9, which is lower than 11.5, then in summer, Qd will exceed Qs, causing a deficit.\n\nCompute Qd_summer at P=9: 38 -2*9=38-18=20.\n\nQs_summer at P=9: -8 +2*9=10.\n\nDeficit=Qd - Qs=20 -10=10.\n\nBut need to confirm if at P=9, which is above 4, producers are willing to produce. Yes, since 9 >4.\n\nSo deficit is 10 units.\n\n2) If no intervention is made, what will be the total expenditures (total revenue) in summer?\n\nIn summer, equilibrium price is 11.5, quantity 15. So total expenditure is 11.5*15=172.5 monetary units.\n\nBut let me compute it properly:\n\nP=11.5, Q=15.\n\n11.5 *15=172.5.\n\nBut the problem might expect an integer. Let's check calculations:\n\n11.5 *15: 10*15=150, 1.5*15=22.5, total 172.5. Yes.\n\nBut maybe we need to present it as a fraction. 11.5 is 23/2, so 23/2 *15=345/2=172.5.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a miscalculation. Wait, let's confirm summer equilibrium price and quantity.\n\nFrom earlier:\n\nSummer demand:38 -2P.\n\nSupply:-8 +2P.\n\nSet equal:38 -2P =-8 +2P \u219238+8=4P \u219246=4P \u2192P=11.5. Correct.\n\nQ=38 -2*11.5=38 -23=15. Correct.\n\nSo total expenditure is 11.5*15=172.5. The problem says \"monetary units of Konyr\". It could be presented as 172.5, but perhaps we need to check if the answer should be in fractions. 172.5 is equivalent to 345/2.\n\n3) At what minimum price will residents refuse to buy \"rawot\"? Does this price depend on the season?\n\nThe minimum price at which residents refuse to buy is the price where Qd=0. This is the choke price of the demand curve. For each season's demand function, set Qd=0 and solve for P.\n\nSince demand varies each season, the choke price (the intercept of the demand curve on the price axis) will vary. Therefore, the minimum price at which residents refuse to buy depends on the season.\n\nLet's compute for each season:\n\nSummer demand: Qd=38 -2P. Set Qd=0 \u219238 -2P=0 \u2192P=19. So residents refuse to buy at P=19 or higher.\n\nSpring demand: Qd=19 -P. Set Qd=0 \u2192P=19. Wait, same as summer? But wait, spring demand is halved. Wait, according to our model, spring demand is Qd=19 -P, which is indeed half of summer's demand function (38 -2P). So the choke price is 19 in both seasons?\n\nWait, that seems odd. Let me check:\n\nSummer demand: Qd=38 -2P. Choke price at Q=0: P=38/2=19.\n\nSpring demand: Qd=19 -P. Choke price: P=19.\n\nSo in both seasons, the choke price is 19. But demand varies each season. However, in our model, spring demand is half of summer demand, but since it's scaled linearly, the choke price remains the same. Wait, that's because if you halve the quantity demanded at each price, the choke price (where Qd=0) remains the same. For example, if summer demand is Q=38 -2P, then halving the demand would be Q=19 -P, which has the same choke price (P=19) but at any price below 19, the quantity demanded is half. However, the maximum price consumers are willing to pay is still 19. Therefore, residents will refuse to buy at P\u226519 in both spring and summer.\n\nBut wait, the problem states that demand varies in each season. So maybe in autumn and winter, the choke price is different. For example, in winter, demand is Qd=9.5 -0.5P. Setting Qd=0: 9.5 -0.5P=0 \u2192P=19. Again, same choke price. So even though demand changes seasonally, the choke price remains 19. Therefore, the minimum price at which residents refuse to buy is 19, regardless of the season. Hence, it does not depend on the season.\n\nBut that seems counterintuitive. How can halving demand result in the same choke price? Because in our model, halving demand was done by scaling the quantity, not by shifting the demand curve. For instance, if demand is Qd = a - bP, halving demand would be Qd = 0.5a - 0.5bP. But in our case, we had summer demand as 38 -2P, spring demand as 19 -P (which is 0.5*(38 -2P)). So the choke price is when P=19 in both cases. Therefore, even though demand is halved, the maximum price consumers are willing to pay remains the same. Thus, the minimum price at which residents refuse to buy is 19, and it does not depend on the season.\n\nBut wait, let's check winter demand. Winter demand is Qd=9.5 -0.5P. Setting Qd=0: 9.5=0.5P \u2192P=19. Same result. Similarly, if autumn has a different demand function, say Qd_autumn = something else, but since the problem states that demand varies each season, but we weren't given info about autumn, perhaps all demand functions, despite varying, have the same choke price? That seems unlikely unless there's a specific reason.\n\nBut according to the way we modeled the demand functions (halving by scaling quantity), the choke price remains the same. Therefore, regardless of the season, the minimum price at which residents refuse to buy is 19, and it does not depend on the season.\n\nHowever, if the demand function had been shifted instead of scaled, the choke price would change. For example, if demand in spring is a parallel shift inward, then the choke price would be lower. But according to our analysis based on the given information, the choke price remains the same.\n\nTherefore, the answers are:\n\n1) Deficit in summer if price frozen at winter price (9) is 10 units.\n\n2) Total expenditures in summer at equilibrium price is 172.5 monetary units.\n\n3) Minimum price where residents refuse to buy is 19, and it does not depend on the season.\n\nBut let me verify the third answer again. If in some season the demand function is different, say, with a different choke price, would that be possible? For example, suppose in some season demand is Qd = 50 -3P, then choke price is 50/3 \u224816.67, which is less than 19. But according to our model, all demand functions have the same choke price of 19. Is that because of the specific way demand was halved in the problem?\n\nThe problem stated that in spring, demand is halved compared to summer. We modeled that as Qd_spring =0.5*Qd_summer. If Qd_summer=38-2P, then Qd_spring=19-P. This maintains the same choke price. If instead, demand was halved by shifting the intercept, like Qd_spring=38 -2P - something, leading to a different choke price, then the answer would differ. But given the problem's phrasing and our interpretation, the choke price remains the same across seasons. Therefore, the minimum price is 19, and it's season-independent.\n\nThus, the final answers are:\n\n1) Deficit of 10 units.\n\n2) Total expenditure of 172.5 monetary units.\n\n3) Minimum price 19, no seasonal dependence.\n\nBut the problem might expect integer answers. Let's check calculations again.\n\nFor question 1, at P=9:\n\nQd_summer=38 -2*9=20\n\nQs_summer=-8 +2*9=10\n\nDeficit=20-10=10. Correct.\n\nQuestion 2: 11.5 *15=172.5. Yes.\n\nQuestion 3: Choke price is 19 in all seasons. Therefore, residents refuse to buy at P\u226519, regardless of season.", "deepseek_solution": "**1) Deficit in Summer with Price Frozen at Winter Price**  \nWhen the price is fixed at the winter equilibrium price of 9 monetary units, the quantity demanded in summer is \\( Q_d = 38 - 2(9) = 20 \\) units, while the quantity supplied is \\( Q_s = -8 + 2(9) = 10 \\) units. The deficit is:  \n\\[\n\\text{Deficit} = Q_d - Q_s = 20 - 10 = \\boxed{10} \\text{ units}.\n\\]\n\n**2) Total Expenditures in Summer Without Intervention**  \nIn summer, the equilibrium price is 11.5 monetary units, and the equilibrium quantity is 15 units. Total expenditures (revenue) are:  \n\\[\n\\text{Expenditure} = P \\times Q = 11.5 \\times 15 = \\boxed{172.5} \\text{ monetary units}.\n\\]\n\n**3) Minimum Price to Refuse Purchase**  \nThe minimum price at which residents refuse to buy \"rawot\" is the choke price of the demand curve, where \\( Q_d = 0 \\). For all seasons, this occurs at:  \n\\[\nP = \\boxed{19} \\text{ monetary units}.\n\\]  \nThis price does not depend on the season, as all seasonal demand functions share the same choke price of 19.", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1) Let's denote the demand function in spring as:\n   \\[\n   Q_{\\text{spring}}^D(p) = a - bp\n   \\]\n   \n   Then, the demand function in winter and summer, respectively, are:\n   \\[\n   Q_{\\text{winter}}^D(p) = \\frac{a - bp}{2}    \n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   Q_{\\text{summer}}^D(p) = 2(a - bp)   \n   \\]\n\n2) The supply function in spring, summer, and autumn is:\n   \\[\n   Q_{\\text{spring/summer/autumn}}^S(p) = c + dp\n   \\]\n\n   And the supply function in winter is:\n   \\[\n   Q_{\\text{winter}}^S(p) = \\frac{c + dp}{2}\n   \\]\n\n3) Noting that equilibrium price during spring and winter are the same as the equilibrium price for both seasons:\n   \\[\n   Q_{\\text{spring}}^D(p) = Q_{\\text{spring/summer/autumn}}^S(p)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   Q_{\\text{winter}}^D(p) = Q_{\\text{winter}}^S(p)\n   \\]\n\n   We set up the equilibrium equations:\n   \\[\n   a - b p_{\\text{spring}} = c + d p_{\\text{spring}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a - b p_{\\text{winter}}}{2} = \\frac{c + d p_{\\text{winter}}}{2}\n   \\]\n   Since the equilibrium prices in spring and winter are the same, we have:\n   \\[\n   p_{\\text{spring}} = p_{\\text{winter}}\n   \\]\n\n4) Given that the quantity sold in spring was two times the quantity sold in winter, and the sales in winter were 5 units, we know:\n   \\[\n   Q_{\\text{spring}} = 10 \\text{ units}\n   \\]\n\n5) Given the total revenue in spring is 90