 = theta and angle YOM = theta.\n\nBut earlier, we assumed angle XOY is theta, but actually, the angle XOY is a given acute angle, say, \u03c6. The problem states that angle XON = angle YOM, but not necessarily equal to \u03c6.\n\nWait, I think I made a mistake earlier. Let's reassign.\n\nLet me clarify:\n\nLet angle XOY be \u03c6 (acute).\n\nPoints M and N are inside angle XOY.\n\nAngle XON = angle YOM = theta, some angle less than \u03c6.\n\nTherefore, angle XON = theta, and angle YOM = theta.\n\nThen, in terms of coordinates:\n\nIf OX is the x-axis and OY makes angle \u03c6 with OX.\n\nPoint N is such that angle XON = theta: tan(theta) = n_y / n_x\n\nPoint M is such that angle YOM = theta. The direction of OY is at angle \u03c6 from OX, so the angle between OY and OM is theta. Therefore, the direction of OM is at angle \u03c6 - theta from OX.\n\nTherefore, tan(phi - theta) = m_y / m_x\n\nSo, m_y / m_x = tan(phi - theta)\n\nThus, we have:\n\nn_y / n_x = tan(theta)\n\nm_y / m_x = tan(phi - theta)\n\nTherefore, m_x = m_y / tan(phi - theta)\n\nSimilarly, n_x = n_y / tan(theta)\n\nThis could relate m_x, m_y, n_x, n_y.\n\nBut how does this help in showing MN' = M''N?\n\nReflect N over OX to get N' = (n_x, -n_y)\n\nReflect M over OY to get M''. Since OY is at angle phi, the reflection is more complex, but if we consider OY as the y-axis in a rotated coordinate system, but this complicates things.\n\nAlternatively, if we consider that reflecting M over OY (at angle phi) results in M'' such that the angle between OY and OM'' is equal to theta, similar to the original condition.\n\nBut this is getting too vague. Given the time I've invested and the lack of progress with coordinates, I think I need to switch tactics.\n\nAnother approach: Since Q is on OX such that \u2220NQO = \u2220MQX, and P is on OY such that \u2220NPO = \u2220MPY, then Q and P are the incenters or excenters of certain triangles, or maybe they lie on the angle bisectors.\n\nAlternatively, consider that Q 