ider ordering the vertices in some order and coloring them one by one, ensuring that each vertex has at most D(G) neighbors already colored when it comes to coloring it, hence having at least one color left.\n\nThis is the greedy coloring algorithm. If we can order the vertices such that each vertex has at most D(G) neighbors preceding it in the order, then using D(G) + 1 colors would suffice. However, such an ordering might not always exist unless we use a specific order.\n\nWait, but in any graph, the maximum degree is D(G). So, if we process the vertices in any order, when we come to color a vertex, it has at most D(G) neighbors, which could have already been colored. But in the worst case, all neighbors could have been colored already, each with a distinct color. Then, we would need a new color. But if we use D(G) + 1 colors, then even if all D(G) neighbors have different colors, there is still one color left. Therefore, the greedy algorithm with any order would use at most D(G) + 1 colors. Wait, is that true?\n\nWait, no, the problem is that the order matters. For example, if you have a star graph, which is a tree with one central node connected to all others. The maximum degree is n - 1. If you color the central node first, then all the leaves can be colored with color 2. But if you color the leaves first, each time you color a leaf, it has only one neighbor (the center) which hasn't been colored yet, so you can color all leaves with color 1, and then the center needs a different color. So in that case, you need 2 colors. But the maximum degree is n - 1, so 2 \u2264 (n - 1) + 1 = n, which holds. But in this case, the order of coloring affects the number of colors used, but the upper bound is still maintained.\n\nWait, but in general, if you use the greedy algorithm with an arbitrary order, the number of colors needed is at most D(G) + 1. Let me confirm this. Suppose you have a graph with maximum degree D. Each vertex has at most D neighbors. When you color the