e vertex. But the greedy argument is perhaps more straightforward.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is that the chromatic number is at most the maximum degree plus one because the greedy coloring algorithm, regardless of the order, will always have a color available for each vertex given that limit on the number of neighbors.", "deepseek_solution": "To demonstrate that the chromatic number \\( \\chi(G) \\) of a graph \\( G \\) satisfies \\( \\chi(G) \\leq D(G) + 1 \\), where \\( D(G) \\) is the maximum degree of \\( G \\), we employ a greedy coloring algorithm:\n\n1. **Greedy Coloring Strategy**:\n   - Order the vertices arbitrarily as \\( v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n \\).\n   - Sequentially assign the smallest available color to each vertex \\( v_i \\) that is not used by its already-colored neighbors.\n\n2. **Key Observation**:\n   - When coloring vertex \\( v_i \\), it has at most \\( D(G) \\) neighbors.\n   - These neighbors can occupy at most \\( D(G) \\) distinct colors.\n   - Since \\( D(G) + 1 \\) colors are available, at least one color remains unused for \\( v_i \\).\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   - Every vertex can be colored without exceeding \\( D(G) + 1 \\) colors.\n   - Thus, \\( \\chi(G) \\leq D(G) + 1 \\).\n\nThis argument holds for any graph, confirming the bound. Exceptions like complete graphs or odd cycles meet the bound exactly, validating its tightness.\n\n**Final Answer**:  \nThe chromatic number of \\( G \\) satisfies \\( \\chi(G) \\leq D(G) + 1 \\), as shown by the greedy coloring algorithm.  \n\\(\\boxed{\\chi(G) \\leq D(G) + 1}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Soit $G$ un graphe et $D(G)$ le degr\u00e9 maximal des sommets de $G$.\n2. Nous devons montrer que le nombre chromatique $\\chi(G)$ est inf\u00e9rieur ou \u00e9gal \u00e0 $D(G) + 1$, c'est-\u00e0-dire, $\\chi(G) \\leq D(G) + 1$.\n3. Consid\u00e9rons le processus de coloration des sommets de $G$ \u00e0 l'aide d'un algorithme glouton. Dans cet algorithme, les sommets du graphe sont colori\u00e9s un par un, en attribuant le plus petit num\u00e9ro de couleur disponible qui ne cr\u00e9e pas de conflit avec les couleurs d\u00e9j\u00e0 attribu\u00e9es aux sommets adjacents.\n4. Soit $M$ le dernier sommet colori\u00e9 par l'algorithme glouton.\n5. Si $M$ n'a pas \u00e9t\u00e9 colori\u00e9 avant, c'est parce que tous ses voisins ont \u00e9t\u00e9 colori\u00e9s avant lui, conform\u00e9ment \u00e0 la proc\u00e9dure de l'algorithme.\n6. Puisque le nombre de voisins d'un sommet est \u00e9gal \u00e0 son degr\u00e9 et que $M$ a \u00e9t\u00e9 colori\u00e9 en dernier, tous ses voisins (au plus $d(M)$ voisins) ont d\u00e9j\u00e0 re\u00e7u une couleur.\n7. En coloriant le sommet $M$ avec une nouvelle couleur qui n'a pas encore \u00e9t\u00e9 utilis\u00e9e par ses voisins, le nombre total de couleurs utilis\u00e9es ne d\u00e9passe pas $d(M) + 1$.\n8. Comme le degr\u00e9 $d(M) \\leq D(G)$, le nombre de couleurs n\u00e9cessaires est alors au plus $D(G) + 1$. Donc, $\\chi(G) \\leq D(G) + 1$.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\chi(G) \\leq D(G) + 1}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the greatest integer value of \\(a\\) for which the equation\n\n\\[\n(x-a)(x-7) + 3 = 0\n\\]\n\nhas at least one integer root.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out the greatest integer value of \\( a \\) such that the equation \\( (x - a)(x - 7) + 3 = 0 \\) has at least one integer root. Hmm, so I need to find \\( a \\) where there's some integer \\( x \\) that satisfies this equation. Let me start by expanding the equation to make it easier to work with.\n\nExpanding the left side: \\( (x - a)(x - 7) = x^2 - 7x - a x + 7a \\). Then adding 3 gives the quadratic equation \\( x^2 - (7 + a)x + 7a + 3 = 0 \\).\n\nSo the quadratic equation is \\( x^2 - (a + 7)x + (7a + 3) = 0 \\). Now, for this quadratic to have at least one integer root, the discriminant must be a perfect square because the roots are given by 