r some integer \\(k\\), leading to:\n\n\\[\na^2 - 14a + 37 = k^2\n\\]\n\nRewriting this as a difference of squares:\n\n\\[\n(a - 7 - k)(a - 7 + k) = 12\n\\]\n\nWe consider all factor pairs of 12, both positive and negative, and check their differences for evenness. The valid factor pairs are (2, 6) and (-2, -6), leading to:\n\n1. For pair (2, 6):\n   - \\(a - 7 - k = 2\\) and \\(a - 7 + k = 6\\)\n   - Solving gives \\(a = 11\\)\n\n2. For pair (-2, -6):\n   - \\(a - 7 - k = -2\\) and \\(a - 7 + k = -6\\)\n   - Solving gives \\(a = 3\\)\n\nAdditionally, substituting the integer root \\(n\\) into the equation \\((n - a)(n - 7) = -3\\) and solving for \\(a\\) yields possible values 3 and 11. The greatest value is 11.\n\nVerification shows that \\(a = 11\\) results in the equation \\((x - 11)(x - 7) + 3 = 0\\) with integer roots 8 and 10.\n\nThus, the greatest integer value of \\(a\\) is \\(\\boxed{11}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo find the largest integer value of \\(a\\) such that the equation\n\n\\[\n(x-a)(x-7) + 3 = 0\n\\]\n\nhas at least one integer root, we need to analyze the given quadratic equation. Let's start by re-arranging the equation in standard quadratic form.\n\n1. Expand the equation:\n   \\[\n   (x-a)(x-7) + 3 = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x^2 - (a+7)x + a \\cdot 7 + 3 = 0\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x^2 - (a+7)x + 7a + 3 = 0\n   \\]\n\n2. For the equation to have an integer solution, the product of the roots \\((x-a)(x-7)\\) plus 3 must yield zero. Hence, we need to check for pairs of integer values \\((x-a)\\) and \\((x-7)\\) whose product equals -3:\n   \\[\n   (x-a)(x-7) = -3\n   \\]\n\n3. Investigate possible pairs of integer values \\((x-a)\\) and \\((x-7)\\):\n   \n   - \\(1 \\times -3 = -3\\)\n   - \\(-1 \\times 3 = -3\\)\n   - \\(3 \\times -1 = -3\\)\n   - \\(-3 \\times 1 = -3\\)\n\n4. Solving for \\(x\\) and \\(a\\) in each case:\n\n   - Case 1:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{cases} \n     x - a = 1 \\\\\n     x - 7 = -3\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n     Solving these equations:\n     \\[\n     x = a + 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = 4\n     \\]\n     Hence:\n     \\[\n     a + 1 = 4 \\implies a = 3\n     \\]\n   \n   - Case 2:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{cases} \n     x - a = -3 \\\\\n     x - 7 = 1\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n     Solving these equations:\n     \\[\n     x = a - 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = 8\n     \\]\n     Hence:\n     \\[\n     a - 3 = 8 \\implies a = 11\n     \\]\n\n   - Case 3:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{cases} \n     x - a = -1 \\\\\n     x - 7 = 3\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n     Solving these equations:\n     \\[\n     x = a + 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = 10\n     \\]\n     Hence:\n     \\[\n     a + 3 = 10 \\implies a = 7\n     \\]\n\n   - Case 4:\n     \\[\n     \\begin{cases} \n     x - a = 3 \\\\\n     x - 7 = -1\n     \\end{cases}\n     \\]\n     Solving these equations:\n     \\[\n     x = a + 3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = 6\n     \\]\n     Hence:\n     \\[\n     a + 3 = 6 \\implies a = 3\n     \\]\n\n5. The possible integer values for \\(a\\) are: \\( \\{3, 11, 7\\} \\). The largest possible value is \\( 11 \\).\n\nConclusion:\n\\[ \\boxed{11} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Vasya solved problems for 10 days - at least one problem each day. Every day (except the first), if the weather was cloudy, he solved one more problem than the previous day, and if it was sunny - one less problem. In the first 9 days, Vasya solved 13 problems. What was the weather like on the tenth day? [5 points]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to tackle this problem step by step. So, Vasya solved problems for 10 days, each day at least one problem. The key points are:\n\n1. From day 2 to day 10, if the weather is cloudy, he solves one more problem than the previous day. If sunny, one less.\n2. The total number of problems solved in the first 9 days is 13.\n3. We need to find the weather on the tenth day.\n\nFirst, let's deno