) \\sim 2x\\), we substitute:\n\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{2x^2}{-2 \\cdot (5x) \\cdot (2x)}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Simplify the Fraction:**\n\n   This reduces to:\n\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{2x^2}{-2 \\cdot 5x \\cdot 2x} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{2x^2}{-20x^2}\n   \\]\n\n   Further simplifying:\n\n   \\[\n   \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{2x^2}{-20x^2} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{2}{-20} = \\lim_{x \\to 0} \\frac{1}{-10} = -\\frac{1}{10}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{1}{10}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given 1980 non-collinear vectors on a plane, any 1979 of these vectors sum to be collinear with another vector outside this set. Prove that the sum of these 1980 vectors is the zero vector.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this problem here: Given 1980 non-collinear vectors on a plane, any 1979 of these vectors sum to be collinear with another vector outside this set. I need to prove that the sum of these 1980 vectors is the zero vector. Hmm, that's interesting. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's parse the problem. We have 1980 vectors in a plane, none of which are collinear. That means no two vectors in the set are scalar multiples of each other. The condition is that if I take any 1979 vectors from this set, their sum is collinear with some other vector that's not in the set. And we need to show that the total sum of all 1980 vectors is zero. \n\nAlright, so the key points here are the non-collinear condition, the property of the sum of any 1979 vectors being collinear with an external vector, and the conclusion that the total sum is zero. Let me start by considering smaller cases to see if I can spot a pattern or a strategy.\n\nSuppose instead of 1980 vectors, we had a smaller number, say 3 vectors. Then the condition would be that any 2 of them sum to a vector collinear with an external one. But I need to see if scaling up this logi