00b7AD)/2.\n\nLet's place the quadrilateral in the coordinate plane. Let\u2019s suppose point A is at (0,0), B at (a,0), D at (0,d), and C at (c,d). Wait, this is a trapezoid, but maybe not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, use vectors. Let\u2019s denote vectors AB, BC, CD, DA. The area of the quadrilateral can be computed as 1/2 | (AB \u00d7 BC) + (BC \u00d7 CD) + (CD \u00d7 DA) + (DA \u00d7 AB) |. But this seems complicated.\n\nAlternatively, the area of a quadrilateral can also be expressed as 1/2 (AC \u00d7 BD), where \u00d7 denotes the cross product. But in 2D, this would translate to 1/2 |AC_x * BD_y - AC_y * BD_x|. However, this is true only if the diagonals are vectors. Wait, no, the area is 1/2 |AC \u00d7 BD| if the diagonals are vectors from the same point. But generally, for any quadrilateral, the area can be computed as 1/2 |AC \u00d7 BD| sin\u03b8, where \u03b8 is the angle between the diagonals. If the diagonals are perpendicular, sin\u03b8=1, so area is 1/2 |AC| |BD|. Therefore, if the diagonals are perpendicular, area is (AC\u00b7BD)/2.\n\nTherefore, if in addition, the quadrilateral is cyclic, then by Ptolemy\u2019s theorem, AB\u00b7CD + BC\u00b7AD = AC\u00b7BD. Therefore, area = (AB\u00b7CD + BC\u00b7AD)/2.\n\nThus, the formula holds for cyclic quadrilaterals with perpendicular diagonals.\n\nTherefore, the correct proof is:\n\n1. For a cyclic quadrilateral, Ptolemy\u2019s theorem holds: AB\u00b7CD + BC\u00b7AD = AC\u00b7BD.\n\n2. If the diagonals AC and BD are perpendicular, then the area is (AC\u00b7BD)/2.\n\n3. Substituting from Ptolemy\u2019s theorem, the area is (AB\u00b7CD + BC\u00b7AD)/2.\n\nTherefore, the formula is valid for cyclic quadrilaterals with perpendicular diagonals.\n\nHowever, the problem statement doesn\u2019t specify these conditions, which suggests either an error in the problem or an unstated assumption. Assuming the problem intended those conditions, the proof follows as above. Otherwise, the formula isn\u2019t generally valid.", "