ub-square (2,2): cells (2,2)=Y, (2,3)=G, (3,2)=B, (3,3). Colors needed: Y, G, B, and one more. Assign (3,3)=R.\n\nSub-square (2,3): cells (2,3)=G, (2,4)=Y, (3,3)=R, (3,4). Colors needed: G, Y, R, one more. Assign (3,4)=B.\n\nSub-square (3,2): cells (3,2)=B, (3,3)=R, (4,2)=Y, (4,3). Colors needed: B, R, Y, one more. Assign (4,3)=G.\n\nSub-square (3,3): cells (3,3)=R, (3,4)=B, (4,3)=G, (4,4). Colors needed: R, B, G, one more. Assign (4,4)=Y.\n\nSo the corners are:\n\n(1,1)=R,\n\n(1,4)=B,\n\n(4,1)=G,\n\n(4,4)=Y.\n\nAgain, all different. Wait, same result. Even though I made different choices in assigning colors along the way, the corners still ended up different. Hmm. Interesting. Is this a coincidence, or is there a deeper reason?\n\nLet me try another path where maybe I try to force a corner to have the same color as another. Suppose I want (1,1)=R and (4,4)=R. Let's see if that's possible.\n\nStarting with (1,1)=R. In sub-square (1,1), assign (1,2)=B, (2,1)=G, (2,2)=Y.\n\nSub-square (1,2): (1,2)=B, (2,2)=Y. So (1,3)=R, (2,3)=G.\n\nSub-square (1,3): (1,3)=R, (2,3)=G. So (1,4)=B, (2,4)=Y.\n\nSub-square (2,1): (2,1)=G, (2,2)=Y. Assign (3,1)=R, (3,2)=B.\n\nSub-square (3,1): (3,1)=R, (3,2)=B. Assign (4,1)=G, (4,2)=Y.\n\nSub-square (2,2): (2,2)=Y, (2,3)=G. Assign (3,2)=B, (3,3)=R.\n\nSub-square (2,3): (2,3)=G, (2,4)=Y. Assign (3,3)=R, (3,4)=B.\n\nSub-square (3,2): (3,2)=B, (3,3)=R. Assign (4,2)=Y, (4,3)=G.\n\nSub-square (3,3): (3,3)=R, (3,4)=B. Assign (4,3)=G, (4,4)=Y.\n\nWait, here (4,4)=Y. Not R. But I wanted (4,4)=R. Let's see if I can adjust.\n\nAlternatively, let's try forcing (4,4)=R. Let's backtrack.\n\nAt sub-square (3,3), which includes (3,3), (3,4), (4,3), (4,4). Suppose we want (4,4)=R. Then the other three cells must be B, G, Y. Let's say (3,3)=B, (3,4)=G, (4,3)=Y. But let's see if this works.\n\nBut earlier steps may conflict. Let's see. After sub-square (3,2): cells (3,2)=B, (3,3)=?, (4,2)=Y, (4,3)=?. In sub-square (3,2), we need all four colors. If (3,2)=B, (4,2)=Y, then (3,3) and (4,3) must be R and G. Suppose we assign (3,3)=R and (4,3)=G. Then, in sub-square (3,3), we have (3,3)=R, (3,4)=?, (4,3)=G, (4,4)=R. Wait, but (4,4)=R would conflict with (3,3)=R in sub-square (3,3). Because the sub-square (3,3) includes (3,3), (3,4), (4,3), (4,4). If both (3,3) and (4,4) are R, that's a conflict. Hence, impossible. Therefore, (4,4) cannot be R if (3,3)=R. So, if we want (4,4)=R, then (3,3) must not be R. Let's try that.\n\nBack to sub-square (3,2): (3,2)=B, (4,2)=Y. Then (3,3) and (4,3) must be R and G. Assign (3,3)=G and (4,3)=R. Then, sub-square (3,3) has (3,3)=G, (4,3)=R. So (3,4) and (4,4) must be B and Y. If we assign (4,4)=R, but (4,3)=R already. Conflict. Wait, no, (4,4) is part of sub-square (3,3) which includes (3,3)=G, (3,4)=B, (4,3)=R, (4,4)=?. So to have all four colors, (4,4) must be Y. Because G, B, R, Y are needed. So (4,4) must be Y. Hence, even if we try to force (4,4)=R, the constraints prevent it.\n\nTherefore, it's impossible to have two corners the same color. This suggests that the corners must all be different.\n\nBut this is just one example. How to generalize?\n\nPerhaps consider that each corner is in a sub-square with three other cells, and through overlapping sub-squares, these colors propagate constraints that eventually force the corners to be different.\n\nAlternatively, use a parity argument or counting.\n\nWait, maybe consider the entire grid. There are four corners, each in their own sub-square. Each sub-square has four distinct colors, so each corner's color is unique within its own sub-square. However, since the sub-squares overlap elsewhere, the colors of the corners must relate to the rest of the grid in a way that prevents duplication.\n\nAlternatively, suppose two corners have the same color. Let's say (1,1)=R and (1,4)=R. Then, in sub-square (1,1), R is at (1,1), and the other three cells must be B, G, Y. In sub-square (1,3), R is at (1,4), so the other three cells must be B, G, Y. Now, consider the cell (2,2). It's part of sub-squares (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (2,2). Each of these sub-squares must have all four colors. But (2,2) is adjacent to many cells. However, without more direct overlap, it's hard to see the conflict.\n\nWait, but maybe looking at the diagonals. If (1,1)=R and (4,4)=R, then in the sub-square (3,3), which includes (4,4), there must be no other R. But (3,3) also includes (3,3), (3,4), (4,3), (4,4). If (4,4)=R, then the other three must be B, G, Y. However, how does this affect (1,1)=R? It might not directly, unless there's a chain of dependencies.\n\nAlternatively, consider that each color must appear exactly four times in the entire grid, since there are 16 cells and four colors. Wait, 16 cells divided by four colors would mean each color appears four times. However, the problem doesn't state that each color must be used exactly four times, only that each 2x2 sub-square has one of each color. So, colors can repeat in different sub-squares, as long as they don't repeat within the same sub-square.\n\nBut maybe, given the overlapping constraints, each color must appear exactly four times. Let me check with my previous example. In the first example, R appeared in (1,1), (1,3), (3,1), (3,3), (4,4) \u2013 wait, no, in the first example, (1,1)=R, (1,3)=R, (3,1)=R, (3,3)=R, and (4,4)=Y. Wait, that's five Rs. But in a 4x4 grid with four colors, each color should appear four times. But in my example, R appeared five times. Wait, that contradicts. Did I make a mistake in counting?\n\nWait, let's go back to the first example:\n\n(1,1)=R\n\n(1,2)=B\n\n(1,3)=R\n\n(1,4)=B\n\n(2,1)=Y\n\n(2,2)=G\n\n(2,3)=Y\n\n(2,4)=G\n\n(3,1)=R\n\n(3,2)=B\n\n(3,3)=R\n\n(3,4)=B\n\n(4,1)=G\n\n(4,2)=Y\n\n(4,3)=G\n\n(4,4)=Y\n\nWait, in this grid, R appears at (1,1), (1,3), (3,1), (3,3) \u2013 four times. Similarly, B at (1,2), (1,4), (3,2), (3,4). G at (2,2), (2,4), (4,1), (4,3). Y at (2,1), (2,3), (4,2), (4,4). So each color appears exactly four times. Okay, so maybe the constraints force each color to appear exactly four times. If that's the case, then the four corners must be four different colors because each color is used exactly four times, and the corners are each in separate sub-squares, so their colors don't conflict. But this is not rigorous.\n\nWait, if each color must appear exactly four times, and the four corners are four cells, then they must each be a different color. But does the problem guarantee that each color appears exactly four times? Or is that a consequence of the 2x2 sub-square constraints?\n\nActually, in the problem statement, it only says that each 2x2 sub-square has one of each color. It doesn't say anything about the frequency of colors in the entire grid. However, in my example, each color appeared exactly four times. Maybe this is necessarily true. Let's see.\n\nThere are 16 cells, four colors. If each 2x2 sub-square contains each color exactly once, then over the nine sub-squares, each color is counted nine times. But each cell is counted in multiple sub-squares. For example, the center cells are in four sub-squares, edge cells in two, and corners in one. So total color counts would be:\n\nLet\u2019s calculate the total number of color occurrences across all sub-squares. Each of the nine sub-squares has four colors, so total color occurrences are 9*4=36. However, each cell is counted in as many sub-squares as it is part of. Corners are in 1, edges in 2, centers in 4. Let's verify:\n\nThe 4x4 grid has:\n\n- 4 corner cells, each in 1 sub-square.\n\n- 8 edge cells (not corners), each in 2 sub-squares.\n\n- 4 center cells, each in 4 sub-squares.\n\nTotal cell contributions: 4*1 + 8*2 + 4*4 = 4 + 16 + 16 = 36, which matches the total color occurrences (9*4=36). Therefore, if each color appears exactly nine times across all sub-squares, but each cell's color is counted multiple times depending on its position.\n\nBut if we let f(c) be the number of times color c appears in the entire grid, then the total color occurrences across all sub-squares would be:\n\nSum over all cells of (number of sub-squares the cell is in) * [color of cell].\n\nBut each sub-square has one of each color, so for each color c, the number of sub-squares containing c is nine (since each sub-square has one of each color). Therefore, each color appears exactly nine times across all sub-squares. However, this counts each cell as many times as the number of sub-squares it is in.\n\nTherefore, for each color c:\n\nSum over all cells of color c of (number of sub-squares the cell is in) = 9.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\nFor color c, let n(c) be the number of cells colored c.\n\nThen, Sum_{cells colored c} (number of sub-squares containing the cell) = 9.\n\nBut the number of sub-squares a cell is in depends on its position:\n\n- Corner cells: 1 sub-square each.\n\n- Edge (non-corner) cells: 2 sub-squares each.\n\n- Center cells: 4 sub-squares each.\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\nLet n_corner(c) = number of corner cells colored c.\n\nn_edge(c) = number of edge (non-corner) cells colored c.\n\nn_center(c) = number of center cells colored c.\n\nThen:\n\nTotal contribution for color c:\n\nn_corner(c)*1 + n_edge(c)*2 + n_center(c)*4 = 9.\n\nSince there are four corners, eight edges, and four centers:\n\nFor each color c:\n\nn_corner(c) \u2264 4,\n\nn_edge(c) \u2264 8,\n\nn_center(c) \u2264 4.\n\nBut also, Sum_{c} n_corner(c) = 4,\n\nSum_{c} n_edge(c) = 8,\n\nSum_{c} n_center(c) = 4.\n\nNow, the equation for each color c:\n\nn_corner(c) + 2n_edge(c) + 4n_center(c) = 9.\n\nWe need to find non-negative integers n_corner(c), n_edge(c), n_center(c) for each color c, such that for each c, the above equation holds, and the sums over all c for corners, edges, centers match the total numbers.\n\nThis seems like a system of equations. Let's see if there's a unique solution.\n\nAssume that the coloring is uniform in some way. Suppose each color appears in exactly the same number of corners, edges, and centers. Since there are four colors, each color would have:\n\nn_corner(c) = 1 (since 4 corners total, 4 colors),\n\nn_edge(c) = 2 (since 8 edges total, 4 colors),\n\nn_center(c) = 1 (since 4 centers total, 4 colors).\n\nThen, for each color c:\n\n1*1 + 2*2 + 1*4 = 1 + 4 + 4 = 9.\n\nWhich matches the equation. Therefore, this is a valid solution. Hence, each color must appear exactly once in the corners, twice on the edges, and once in the center. Therefore, the four corner cells must all be different colors. Because each color appears exactly once in the corners.\n\nTherefore, this proves that the four corner cells must have different colors.\n\nYes, this seems like a valid argument. By considering the total contributions of each color across all sub-squares, and given the structure of the grid, each color must appear exactly once in the corners, hence the four corners are all different colors.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that the four corner cells of the 4\u00d74 grid must be colored differently under the given constraints, we analyze the color distribution across overlapping sub-squares and utilize a counting argument:\n\n1. **Sub-square Constraints**: Each 2\u00d72 sub-square contains all four colors. There are nine such sub-squares, and each cell participates in 1, 2, or 4 sub-squares depending on its position (corners: 1, edges: 2, centers: 4).\n\n2. **Total Color Count**: Across all sub-squares, each color must appear exactly nine times (since each sub-square has one of each color, and there are nine sub-squares). This gives the equation for each color \\( c \\):\n   \\[\n   n_{\\text{corner}}(c) + 2n_{\\text{edge}}(c) + 4n_{\\text{center}}(c) = 9\n   \\]\n   where \\( n_{\\text{corner}}(c) \\), \\( n_{\\text{edge}}(c) \\), and \\( n_{\\text{center}}(c) \\) denote the number of corner, edge, and center cells colored \\( c \\), respectively.\n\n3. **Uniform Distribution**: Given there are 4 corners, 8 edges, and 4 centers in total, the only solution satisfying the equation for all four colors is:\n   - Each color appears **exactly once** in the corners (\\( n_{\\text{corner}}(c) = 1 \\)),\n   - **Twice** on the edges (\\( n_{\\text{edge}}(c) = 2 \\)),\n   - **Once** in the centers (\\( n_{\\text{center}}(c) = 1 \\)).\n\n4. **Conclusion**: Since each color must occupy exactly one corner cell to satisfy the global distribution requirement, all four corner cells must be distinct colors.\n\n**Final Answer**: The four corner cells of the 4\u00d74 grid must all be colored differently.  \n\\boxed{\\text{The four corner cells must each be a different color.}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe will use coordinates to designate the squares in a 4 by 4 grid: rows will be labeled $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$ from top to bottom, and columns will be labeled $1$, $2$, $3$, $4$ from left to right.\n\n1. **Assume two adjacent corners are the same color:**\n\n    - Suppose the top-left corner $A1$ and the top-right corner $A4$ are the same color, say green. We will show that this assumption leads to a contradiction.\n    - Since $A1$ is green, $A2$ and $B2$ cannot be green because each 2 by 2 sub-square must have all four colors.\n        \\[\n        \\text{Hence, } A2 \\neq \\text{green and } B2 \\neq \\text{green.}\n        \\]\n    - Similarly, since $A4$ is green, $A3$ and $B3$ cannot be green:\n        \\[\n        \\text{Hence, } A3 \\neq \\text{green and } B3 \\neq \\text{green.}\n        \\]\n    - This creates a situation where the sub-square $A2$, $A3$, $B2$, $B3$ does not have a square colored green, which contradicts our initial condition.\n\n2. **Assume two opposite corners are the same color:**\n\n    - Suppose the top-left corner $A1$ and the bottom-right corner $D4$ are the same color, say green. We will show that this assumption also leads to a contradiction.\n    - Consider the middle sub-square involving $B2$, $B3$, $C2$, and $C3$. Since it must contain a green cell, one of these four cells must be green. Without loss of generality, assume $B3$ is green.\n        \\[\n        \\text{Let } B3 = \\text{green.}\n        \\]\n    - Now, the sub-square involving $B1$, $B2$, $C1$, and $C2$ must also contain a green cell. Since $B3$ is green, $C1$ must be green to ensure one green in that sub-square.\n        \\[\n        \\text{Let } C1 = \\text{green.}\n        \\]\n    - Next, the sub-square involving $C2$, $C3$, $D2$, and $D3$ must also have green. Since $C1$ is green and to keep the distribution balanced, place a green cell in $D2$.\n        \\[\n        \\text{Let } D2 = \\text{green.}\n        \\]\n    - However, now the sub-square involving $C1$, $C2$, $D1$, and $D2$ contains two green cells ($C1$ and $D2$), which contradicts our requirement for one of each color per sub-square.\n\nThus, the two conditions, adjacently placed corners or diagonally opposite corners being the same color, both lead to contradictions.\n\n3. **Conclusion from contradictions:**\n\n    - Since neither adjacent nor opposite corners can be the same color, and no corner can share its color with another, all four corners must be uniquely colored to satisfy the condition that every 2 by 2 sub-square within the 4 by 4 grid consists of exactly one of each color.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the four corner cells in the large 4 by 4 square must be colored differently.\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The four corner cells must have different colors}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Compute the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\sin ^{6}\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right) \\cos ^{2}\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right) d x\n$$", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, I need to compute the definite integral of sin^6(x/4) times cos^2(x/4) from 0 to 2\u03c0. Hmm, let's see. Integrals involving powers of sine and cosine can sometimes be tricky. I remember that there are some trigonometric identities that can help simplify these kinds of expressions. Maybe using power-reduction formulas or double-angle identities? Let me think.\n\nFirst, the integrand is sin^6(x/4) cos^2(x/4). Both functions have arguments of x/4, so maybe I can make a substitution to simplify the integral. Let me try substituting t = x/4. Then, dt = dx/4, which means dx = 4 dt. When x is 0, t is 0, and when x is 2\u03c0, t is (2\u03c0)/4 = \u03c0/2. So the integral becomes 4 times the integral from 0 to \u03c0/2 of sin^6(t) cos^2(t) dt. That might be easier to handle.\n\nNow, the integral is 4 \u222b\u2080^{\u03c0/2} sin^6 t cos^2 t dt. How do I approach this? For integrals of the form sin^n t cos^m t, if the exponents are even numbers, we can use power-reduction identities. Alternatively, maybe using beta functions or gamma functions? But since this is a definite integral from 0 to \u03c0/2, there's a formula called the beta function which relates to integrals of sin^n t cos^m t over this interval. The formula is:\n\n\u222b\u2080^{\u03c0/2} sin^n t cos^m t dt = (1/2) B((n+1)/2, (m+1)/2)\n\nwhere B is the beta function. And the beta function is related to the gamma function by B(a,b) = \u0393(a)\u0393(b)/\u0393(a+b). So if I can express the integral in terms of gamma functions, maybe I can compute it.\n\nAlternatively, I can use the reduction formulas for sine and cosine powers. Let me recall. For even exponents, it's often helpful to use the double-angle identity. Let me see. Since both exponents are even (6 and 2), I can express sin^6 t as (sin^2 t)^3 and cos^2 t as (cos^2 t). Then use the identity sin^2 t = (1 - cos 2t)/2 and cos^2 t = (1 + cos 2t)/2. Let me try that.\n\nSo, sin^6 t cos^2 t = [(1 - cos 2t)/2]^3 * [(1 + cos 2t)/2]. Let's expand this out. First, expand [(1 - cos 2t)/2]^3:\n\n(1 - cos 2t)^3 / 8. Then multiply by (1 + cos 2t)/2, so overall, we have (1 - cos 2t)^3 (1 + cos 2t) / 16.\n\nLet me compute (1 - cos 2t)^3 (1 + cos 2t). Let's first expand (1 - cos 2t)^3. Let's expand the cube:\n\n(1 - cos 2t)^3 = 1 - 3 cos 2t + 3 cos^2 2t - cos^3 2t.\n\nThen multiply this by (1 + cos 2t):\n\nMultiply term by term:\n\n1*(1 + cos 2t) = 1 + cos 2t\n\n-3 cos 2t*(1 + cos 2t) = -3 cos 2t - 3 cos^2 2t\n\n3 cos^2 2t*(1 + cos 2t) = 3 cos^2 2t + 3 cos^3 2t\n\n- cos^3 2t*(1 + cos 2t) = -cos^3 2t - cos^4 2t\n\nCombine all terms:\n\n1 + cos 2t -3 cos 2t -3 cos^2 2t +3 cos^2 2t +3 cos^3 2t -cos^3 2t -cos^4 2t\n\nSimplify term by term:\n\n1 + (cos2t -3cos2t) + (-3cos\u00b22t +3cos\u00b22t) + (3cos\u00b32t -cos\u00b32t) -cos\u20742t\n\nSimplify each group:\n\n1 - 2cos2t + 0 + 2cos\u00b32t - cos\u20742t\n\nSo the product is 1 - 2cos2t + 2cos\u00b32t - cos\u20742t.\n\nTherefore, the integrand becomes [1 - 2cos2t + 2cos\u00b32t - cos\u20742t]/16.\n\nSo the integral 4 \u222b\u2080^{\u03c0/2} sin^6 t cos^2 t dt becomes 4*(1/16) \u222b\u2080^{\u03c0/2} [1 - 2cos2t + 2cos\u00b32t - cos\u20742t] dt\n\nSimplify the constants: 4/16 = 1/4. So it's (1/4) times the integral of [1 - 2cos2t + 2cos\u00b32t - cos\u20742t] dt from 0 to \u03c0/2.\n\nNow, let's split the integral into four separate integrals:\n\n(1/4) [ \u222b\u2080^{\u03c0/2} 1 dt - 2 \u222b\u2080^{\u03c0/2} cos2t dt + 2 \u222b\u2080^{\u03c0/2} cos\u00b32t dt - \u222b\u2080^{\u03c0/2} cos\u20742t dt ]\n\nLet's compute each integral one by one.\n\nFirst integral: \u222b\u2080^{\u03c0/2} 1 dt = [t]\u2080^{\u03c0/2} = \u03c0/2 - 0 = \u03c0/2.\n\nSecond integral: \u222b\u2080^{\u03c0/2} cos2t dt. Let me make substitution u = 2t, du = 2 dt, so dt = du/2. When t=0, u=0; t=\u03c0/2, u=\u03c0. So integral becomes (1/2) \u222b\u2080^{\u03c0} cos u du = (1/2)[sin u]\u2080^{\u03c0} = (1/2)(0 - 0) = 0. Wait, sin \u03c0 is 0 and sin 0 is 0. So the second integral is 0.\n\nThird integral: \u222b\u2080^{\u03c0/2} cos\u00b32t dt. Hmm, integrating cos^3. Let's recall that for odd powers of cosine, we can use substitution. Let me use the identity cos^3\u03b8 = (1 - sin^2\u03b8)cos\u03b8. Let me set \u03b8 = 2t.\n\nSo, cos\u00b32t = (1 - sin\u00b22t)cos2t. Then, let me substitute u = sin2t, du = 2cos2t dt. Then, cos2t dt = du